//! THE SLAB BUDGET — how many of each object this kernel can hold, the stride
//! it reserves per thread context, and the sentinel each table answers with
//! when it has no slot to give.
//!
//! ONE MODULE BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE DECISION. Every number here is a fixed-size
//! table's dimension, they are chosen against each other (`MAX_ATTACHMENTS` is
//! the SUM of the waitables, because an attachment is one (Waiter, waitable)
//! pair and §2.2 allows a handle at most one Waiter), and raising one is meant
//! to be an edit in one place. lib.saw already had them as one adjacent block;
//! this file is that block.
//!
//! IT IS ALSO WHAT THE LANGUAGE ALLOWS TODAY (DF-232g): a `static` DERIVED from
//! an imported const is not a compile-time length, so a derived size and its
//! operands cannot be split across modules — `MAX_ATTACHMENTS` has to sit
//! beside the four it adds up.
//!
//! AUDIT (design 1, M3 unit 1.5): NO SLAB SCAN OR HANDLE WALK IN THE KERNEL
//! TAKES A PREEMPTION POINT, and this file is where that verdict belongs
//! because these numbers ARE its argument. Every free-slot search, every
//! by-line/by-key lookup and every teardown sweep is bounded by one of the
//! constants below — 8, 16, 20 at the largest — over an O(1) body in RAM, so
//! there is no masked window here for a point to shorten. Raising one of these
//! numbers by an order of magnitude is what would make the audit worth
//! re-running.

import hal
import sosabi.{ClockType}

/// Processes the kernel can hold. THREE since M4 unit 5 (sawos design 21,
/// user-ruled Sep 2): root, and the two children driver-as-service needs — a
/// driver child that owns the UART and a client child that reads it through a
/// pipe. It was TWO from M3 unit 2 (sawos design 2 D-6) to there.
///
/// **"RAISING THIS NUMBER IS THE ONLY EDIT" HAS NOW BEEN EXERCISED TWICE, and
/// it held both times.** Every table that is per-process was already indexed
/// rather than assumed — the handle table is a slice of `HANDLES` at
/// `p * MAX_HANDLES`, the thread/event/waiter/interrupt/timer slabs carry a
/// `process` field their teardown scans by — so the one-to-two step was this
/// line and the `ProcessSlot` arithmetic that follows from it, and the
/// two-to-three step was this line and NOTHING ELSE in the kernel. What did NOT
/// come for free at the first step is what design 2 is about: the protection
/// domain, which one process could leave installed and two cannot (D-5), and
/// the end of a process, which with one was the end of the machine (D-6).
///
/// **THE CENSUS THE SECOND BUMP OWES, since a slot is not free** (design 21's
/// forcing question, answered by measurement rather than by assertion):
///
///   - `MAX_HANDLES` handle entries — `HANDLES` grows 32 -> 48 rows, and
///     `BOOT_HANDLES` with it. Zero-initialised, so it is .bss and no image
///     byte is spent on it (design 149).
///   - one `MAX_GRANT_ROWS`-row grant record, one `ProcessSlot`, one
///     `QUOTA_KINDS`-wide pair of quota rows.
///   - one term in `MAX_ATTACHMENTS` below (22 -> 23), because a Process handle
///     is a waitable and one attachment per slot is the honest bound.
///   - **NOT the protection-row budget.** Protection rows are PER DOMAIN and
///     reprogrammed at `load_domain`, so `hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` is what ONE
///     process may hold installed and a third process adds nothing to it.
///     Root's own record is the one to watch, and it does not move: root spends
///     `IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS` = 3 (its two loadable segments plus the kernel's
///     stack grant) whatever a launch wires up, because a launcher hands out
///     CAPABILITIES and maps nothing — `process_create` reads a child's image
///     with kernel privilege and a driver child maps its own window. Design 21
///     measured a two-child launch at 3 rows used, leaving every profile's
///     budget five or more rows to spare.
///
/// Three rather than eight for the same reason it was two rather than eight:
/// the number is the smallest that serves the workload of record. A fourth is
/// an edit to this line when a unit argues for one.
public(package) static MAX_PROCESSES: Int = 3

/// Root's slot. The kernel loads exactly one image (§12) and it lands here, so
/// "process 0" and "root" are the same statement — which is what lets
/// `end_process` fork on a comparison rather than on a flag.
public(package) static ROOT_PROCESS: Int = 0

/// "No process". The plain-form sentinel, like `NO_THREAD`.
public(package) static NO_PROCESS: Int = -1

/// Memory objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5; sawos design 2 D-2, raised
/// by design 6 D-7).
///
/// **IT IS A REAL ALLOCATION BUDGET NOW, which is what unit 4 changed about
/// it.** Unit 2's number was a census of boot regions — v1 minted these only at
/// boot, one per row of the build-emitted region table, and nothing created one
/// at run time. `MemoryOp.Split` creates one per cut, so the slab is what a
/// pool is spent THROUGH: sixteen is the boot rows plus room for a handful of
/// derived regions, which is what a hand-written system's launcher needs.
///
/// It is deliberately NOT large, and since M3 unit 5 it is the SLAB that stays
/// small rather than the system that stays short. Free-on-last-reference landed
/// (sawos design 7 D-1), so a cut that is dropped returns its slot inside the
/// release and this number bounds CONCURRENT regions rather than total cuts —
/// `tests/memory-split` walks twenty rounds past these sixteen slots with
/// nothing refused.
///
/// **AND SINCE M5 UNIT 5 IT IS NOT THE POOL EITHER** (sawos design 28). What
/// this paragraph used to say — that a long-running allocator meets the POOL,
/// because a freed Memory returns its slot and not its BYTES — was the honest
/// state of the system and is what that unit built. A dropped region's range
/// goes back onto its pool root's free list now, so what bounds a workload is
/// concurrency in both currencies: how many regions exist at once, and how many
/// bytes are out at once.
public(package) static MAX_MEMORIES: Int = 16

/// The same sentinel for the Memory slab.
public(package) static NO_MEMORY: Int = -1

/// Free-range NODES a pool's free list can hold, over ALL pools (sawos design
/// 28; design 25 D-5).
///
/// **THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE IS WHAT THIS NUMBER RETIRES.** `MAX_MEMORIES` used to
/// say a long-running allocator meets the POOL rather than the slab, because a
/// freed region returned its slot and not its BYTES. It returns both now: the
/// range goes onto its pool root's free list, and this is the storage that list
/// is made of.
///
/// A NODE IS ONLY EVER NEEDED FOR A HOLE. A range returned ADJACENT to another
/// free range coalesces into it, and one adjacent to the pool's own unspent
/// frontier folds back into the cursor and needs no node at all — which is the
/// common shape, since a cut-and-drop loop returns the piece it just took. So
/// the list is as long as the pool is FRAGMENTED, and thirty-two is generous
/// against a Memory slab of sixteen: every live region could be released out of
/// order, each landing as its own hole, and the list would still be half empty.
///
/// **EXHAUSTION MUST NOT FAULT A RELEASE** (design 28's infallible-release
/// doctrine). A range that finds no node after coalescing is LEAKED, honestly
/// and counted, into its pool's `dropped` — never refused, never silently lost.
/// `SlabDonate` (design 25 D-6) names this slab as its first customer.
public(package) static MAX_FREE_RANGES: Int = 32

/// The same sentinel for the free-range slab.
public(package) static NO_FREE_RANGE: Int = -1

/// IoMemory objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-1/D-7).
///
/// SMALLER THAN THE RAM SLAB, and for the reason `MAX_INTERRUPTS` is small: an
/// IoMemory is a DEVICE WINDOW, and a machine has as many of those as it has
/// peripherals a driver wants — a board fact, not a workload one. Carving is
/// non-exclusive, so several windows onto one peripheral cost several slots,
/// which is what makes eight rather than four.
public(package) static MAX_IOMEMORIES: Int = 8

/// The same sentinel for the IoMemory slab.
public(package) static NO_IOMEMORY: Int = -1

/// Mapping objects the kernel can hold (spec §2.5; sawos design 6 D-3/D-7).
///
/// A Mapping is one INSTALLED ROW with a handle on it, so the honest ceiling is
/// the rows every process's domain can hold at once — which is smaller than
/// this on one profile and the same order on the other. Eight is that, and a
/// map that meets it answers `NoResource` exactly as one that meets the
/// hardware's row budget does.
public(package) static MAX_MAPPINGS: Int = 8

/// The same sentinel for the Mapping slab.
public(package) static NO_MAPPING: Int = -1

/// Rows in a process's GRANT RECORD (sawos design 2 D-5) — the protection map
/// the scheduler REPLAYS when it switches into that process.
///
/// It is the ARRAY LENGTH, and since M3 unit 4 that is ALL it is. One row per
/// image segment plus one for the kernel's stack grant was the whole story
/// while grants were made at load time only; `MemoryOp.Map` installs rows at
/// run time, so what a process may actually hold is a PER-PROFILE budget the
/// HAL publishes (`hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET`) and this number is the storage that
/// has to cover the larger of them.
///
/// **THE TWO PROFILES' BUDGETS NOW MEAN DIFFERENT THINGS**, and that is the
/// note unit 4 owes this constant. One profile's wall is HARDWARE — a fixed
/// number of numbered protection regions, eight of them since design 6 D-4
/// widened the budget — so a map meeting it is a map the machine cannot
/// install. The other has no such limit at all (it grants by page table) and
/// its wall is this POLICY number, chosen so that one array serves both. A map
/// checks the HAL's number and never this one; this one is what stops the array
/// from being too short for it (the assert below).
///
/// It stays 9 because raising it costs every process slot a row of storage and
/// nothing needs more yet.
public(package) static MAX_GRANT_ROWS: Int = 9

static_assert(hal.MAX_ROOT_SEGMENTS + 1 <= 9,
              "a process's grant record must hold every segment plus its stack")

static_assert(hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET <= 9,
              "a process's grant record must hold every row its profile may install")

// EVERY PROCESS SLOT NEEDS A PROTECTION DOMAIN TO LIVE IN (sawos design 27).
// On a tier that keeps a persistent table set per process, the HAL sizes a
// static pool and advertises how many sets it holds; a process slot with no set
// behind it would index past the pool. The number is declared in the HAL rather
// than read from here because a HAL that had to import the kernel to size its
// own page tables would be the wrong way round — this assert is what keeps the
// two spellings honest, exactly as the `GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` pair above does.
static_assert(MAX_PROCESSES <= hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS,
              "every process slot needs a protection domain in the HAL's pool")

/// Threads the kernel can hold, across all processes.
public(package) static MAX_THREADS: Int = 8

/// Events the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2.4).
public(package) static MAX_EVENTS: Int = 8

/// Waiters the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2.2). Fewer than
/// events on purpose: a Waiter is a DISPATCH POINT, and the shape §2.2 ratifies
/// — many handles per Waiter, many threads per Waiter — is one per pool of
/// workers rather than one per notification.
public(package) static MAX_WAITERS: Int = 4

/// Interrupts the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2 table, §9).
///
/// Small because an Interrupt is a BOUND DEVICE LINE, and a machine has as many
/// of those as it has devices with drivers — not as many as it has
/// notifications. Every one costs a masked/unmasked line in the controller,
/// which is a real resource the board fixes.
public(package) static MAX_INTERRUPTS: Int = 4

/// Clock objects in the system (spec §2 table; design 232 unit 1, amended
/// Aug 17).
///
/// NOT A SLAB BUDGET — A CENSUS OF DOMAINS. A hardware-backed Clock is ONE
/// KERNEL-ETERNAL OBJECT PER `ClockType`, existing from boot and owned by
/// nobody: a machine has one monotonic counter, and a per-process object naming
/// it would be a copy of a fact with a lifetime attached to it. So the slab has
/// exactly one slot per domain, THE SLOT IS THE DOMAIN'S ORDINAL, and nothing is
/// ever allocated or freed here. v1 declares one domain, hence one.
///
/// This is what a VIRTUAL clock will NOT be: a virtual clock is a separately
/// created, STATEFUL object (it has an offset, a rate, an owner) and it will get
/// its own creation op and its own lifetime. Sharing this slab with it would put
/// two different kinds of thing in one table for the sake of a name they share.
public(package) static CLOCK_KINDS: Int = 1

/// Timers the kernel can hold, across all processes (spec §2 table; design 232
/// unit 1).
///
/// Sized like Events rather than like Interrupts: a Timer is a per-DEADLINE
/// object — one per timeout a process is tracking — not a per-device one, and
/// the shape M4's select-with-timeout makes is one Timer beside each thing
/// being waited on.
public(package) static MAX_TIMERS: Int = 8

/// Attachments the kernel can hold, across all Waiters.
///
/// Sized to cover every waitable being attached at once, which is the honest
/// bound: an attachment is one (Waiter, waitable) pair and §2.2 allows a handle
/// at most one Waiter, so nothing can need more than there are waitables. The
/// Timer is the third waitable kind and joins the sum for that reason — a
/// Clock does NOT, because a Clock is not waitable.
///
/// **AND `MAX_PROCESSES` IS THE FOURTH TERM** (sawos design 8, M3 unit 5.5).
/// A Process handle is a waitable now, one attachment per process slot at
/// most, so the sum grows by the process table's own size and by exactly that.
/// A THREAD is still not waitable, which is why `MAX_THREADS` is absent — the
/// same reason the Clock is. The M4-unit-5 process bump therefore moved this
/// number too, 22 -> 23, by the arithmetic and not by an edit — which is what
/// the derived spelling is for.
///
/// **THE PIPE WAITABLES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT TERMS**, and the omission is a
/// judgement rather than an oversight: an outlet, an inlet, a claim and an
/// obligation are all attachable since M4 unit 3, and sizing this sum to cover
/// every one of them at once would multiply the table by the in-flight budget
/// for a shape no program in the tree has. `NoResource` at `add` is what says
/// so when a program finds the wall — design 21's driver holds at most six
/// attachments at once (its outlet, its IRQ line, and up to four held reads).
public(package) static MAX_ATTACHMENTS: Int = (MAX_EVENTS + MAX_INTERRUPTS
                                               + MAX_TIMERS + MAX_PROCESSES)

/// Pipe CONNECTIONS the kernel can hold (spec §2.1; sawos design 13).
///
/// **ONE SLAB FOR THE PAIR, NOT ONE PER END** (design 10 D-1): a `PipeInlet` and
/// a `PipeOutlet` are two counted KINDS over one connection slot, so this is a
/// census of live connections rather than of endpoints. A connection frees when
/// BOTH of its reference columns reach zero.
///
/// Four rather than eight because a slot is not free: it carries
/// `PIPE_INFLIGHT` staging slots of `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` each, which is the only
/// per-object storage in the kernel that is measured in kilobytes. Sized like
/// `MAX_WAITERS` and for a related reason — a connection is a per-CLIENT
/// relationship, and a hand-written system has a handful.
///
/// **UNIT 5 WAS EXPECTED TO ARGUE FOR MORE AND DID NOT** (sawos design 21).
/// Driver-as-service serves TWO clients over ONE connection — sibling inlets
/// are per-client authority, which is exactly why an endpoint kept `Mint` when
/// the one-shots lost it (`designs/010` ruling 12(c)) — so a service's
/// connection count tracks its number of SESSIONS and not its number of
/// clients. The three-process delegation beside it spends two, which is the
/// largest a case in this tree has wanted.
public(package) static MAX_PIPES: Int = 4

/// The same sentinel for the Pipe slab.
public(package) static NO_PIPE: Int = -1

/// **MESSAGES ONE CONNECTION MAY HOLD STAGED, AS A BUILD DEFINE** (spec §2.1 as
/// amended by design 10 ruling 6; sawos design 13).
///
/// **IT DERIVES FROM `MAX_THREADS`, AND THAT IS THE RULING'S OWN ARITHMETIC**:
/// two per thread, so a system in which every thread has a request in flight and
/// a reply outstanding still cannot fill the ring on the sender's own account.
/// A derived size beside the number it derives from is what DF-232g permits and
/// what this file exists for.
///
/// **IT AMENDS §2.1's "STAGES ONE MESSAGE IN A FIXED SLOT"** (ruling 6): the
/// staging is a small per-connection RING of fixed slots, which keeps the
/// property that sentence was ratified FOR — zero dynamic kernel allocation —
/// while letting a client post again before the server has taken the first.
///
/// It is deliberately NOT published to userspace, and that is the cut design 13
/// records against ruling 6: a ring's depth is a slab dimension, and a caller
/// that meets a full one is told `WouldBlock` without ever needing the number.
/// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` is the half that IS a published contract, so it lives in
/// `sosabi` where both halves compile against one declaration.
public(package) static PIPE_INFLIGHT: Int = 2 * MAX_THREADS

static_assert(PIPE_INFLIGHT >= 1,
              "a connection must be able to stage at least one message")
static_assert(PIPE_INFLIGHT <= 64,
              "the staging ring is inline per connection — keep the storage bounded")

/// Handles one process's table can hold (§3).
public(package) static MAX_HANDLES: Int = 16

/// Bytes reserved per thread for its saved context.
///
/// A STRIDE, not a size: the frame layout is the HAL's and differs per profile
/// (32 words on one, 34 doublewords on the other), so the kernel reserves a
/// generous power-of-two slot and asserts the machine's real frame fits. The
/// stride is also the ALIGNMENT the arena is rounded to, which is how a frame
/// gets the 16-byte alignment one profile's stack pointer requires without the
/// kernel having to know that is why.
public(package) static THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE: Int = 512

static_assert(hal.FRAME_BYTES <= THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE,
              "this machine's trap frame does not fit the kernel's per-thread stride")

static_assert(THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE % 16 == 0,
              "a frame slot must be 16-byte aligned on every profile")

/// One extra stride of slack, so rounding the arena's base up to the stride
/// cannot run the last slot past the end.
public(package) static FRAME_ARENA_WORDS: Int = (THREAD_FRAME_STRIDE / 8) * (MAX_THREADS + 1)

static_assert(CLOCK_KINDS == 1,
              "one slot per ClockType, and v1 declares exactly one domain")

/// "No thread". Slot indices are ordinary 0-based `Int`s in the tables and
/// PLUS-ONE inside list links; this is the sentinel for the plain form.
public(package) static NO_THREAD: Int = -1

/// The same sentinel for the other tables. There is none for CLOCKS: every
/// domain's slot exists from boot, so "no clock" is not a state the kernel can
/// be in.
public(package) static NO_EVENT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_WAITER: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_INTERRUPT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_ATTACHMENT: Int = -1

public(package) static NO_TIMER: Int = -1

// =============================================================================
// THE QUOTA DEFAULTS (spec §12's promise; sawos design 7 D-3)
// =============================================================================
//
// **THEY ARE IN THIS FILE BECAUSE THEY ARE THE SAME DECISION THE SLAB SIZES
// ARE.** A slab size says how many of a thing the MACHINE can hold; a default
// says how many one CHILD may hold of that. Choosing one without seeing the
// other is how a policy cap ends up above the table it is supposed to sit
// under, so the two blocks are adjacent and every number below is visibly
// smaller than the slab above it.
//
// **THEY ARE THE CHILD'S NUMBERS AND NOBODY ELSE'S.** Root is init and its
// policy cap IS the machine (D-3), so root's rows are `QUOTA_UNLIMITED` and the
// SLAB is what refuses it — see `install_quota_limits` in `kcore.objects` for
// why a numerically-equal cap would have been the wrong spelling. There is no
// per-create quota argument in v1: a launcher gets these numbers for every
// child it makes, and the vocabulary for assigning others arrives with the
// "assigned by policy" sentence the `process_create` receiver ruling left open.
//
// **A THREAD ROW IS CHARGED AND NEVER CREDITED BEFORE TEARDOWN**, and that is
// not an oversight: a thread slot itself is only returned at its process's
// teardown (`ThreadState.Exited` is a state a slot stays in, so a join arriving
// late still finds the exit code), so the quota tracks the slab exactly. A
// process that creates and exits four threads has spent its four, which is what
// the machine would have told it one slab larger.

/// Threads a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_THREADS: Int = 4

/// Events a child may hold at once (spec §2.4).
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_EVENTS: Int = 4

/// Waiters a child may hold at once. Fewer than events for the reason
/// `MAX_WAITERS` is: a Waiter is a DISPATCH POINT, one per pool of workers.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_WAITERS: Int = 2

/// IRQ lines a child may hold bound at once. Small because a driver child
/// drives a device, not a workload.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_INTERRUPTS: Int = 2

/// Timers a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_TIMERS: Int = 4

/// RAM regions a child may hold at once — its own, plus what it has split.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_MEMORIES: Int = 4

/// Device windows a child may hold at once.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_IOMEMORIES: Int = 2

/// GRANT ROWS a child's domain may carry beyond the ones its image spent.
///
/// **THIS ROW COUNTS INSTALLED ROWS, NOT MAPPING OBJECTS**, which is the one
/// place design 7's ledger departs from "quotas count objects" — and it is what
/// makes agenda item 8's assert executable. See `QuotaKind.Mapping`.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS: Int = 2

/// Pipe CONNECTIONS a child may hold at once (spec §2.1; sawos design 13).
///
/// **CREATOR-PAYS, so this row counts what a child MADE and not what it was
/// handed** (design 10 ruling 6's "the pair's creator is charged for both
/// ends"). A child given one end of a connection its launcher created spends
/// nothing here — it spends a HANDLE row, like every other capability that
/// arrives — which is what makes a server child able to hold many sessions
/// under a small pipe budget.
///
/// Two, on `DEFAULT_QUOTA_WAITERS`' reasoning: a connection is a per-client
/// relationship and a child that makes its own is a launcher in miniature.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_PIPES: Int = 2

/// Handle-table entries a child may hold at once (§3; design 3's promised row).
///
/// Below `MAX_HANDLES` on purpose, so a child meets ITS budget rather than the
/// table's — the table is shared machinery and the number a child is told about
/// should be its own.
public(package) static DEFAULT_QUOTA_HANDLES: Int = 12

/// Grant rows a v1 image spends before its process runs: `code|rodata|rw`
/// collapses to two loadable segments, plus the kernel's stack grant at the
/// region's top. Root's images and children's images both spend three.
public(package) static IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS: Int = 3

/// The SMALLER of the two profiles' row budgets, written down so the assert
/// below can be a compile-time one. `hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET` is a `UInt` and the
/// quota numbers are `Int`s; rather than cast in a `static_assert`, the minimum
/// is stated here and pinned against the HAL on the next line.
public(package) static MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET: Int = 8

static_assert(hal.GRANT_ROW_BUDGET >= 8,
              "MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET is stale — this profile grants fewer rows than it claims")

// **AGENDA ITEM 8's ASSERT, THE COMPILE-TIME HALF** (sawos design 7 D-3). The
// mapping default is chosen so that a child on it can always spend its whole
// budget without the MACHINE refusing first — on either profile, since the
// smaller of the two is what is tested. That is what makes `install_row`'s
// `fatal` a statement about a kernel bug rather than about a number somebody
// picked too large: a `map()` meeting the physical wall with quota headroom
// left cannot be a policy mistake if policy was proven to fit.
//
// The RUNTIME half is `install_quota_limits`, which clamps every process's
// mapping row to what its own domain actually has left after its image — the
// part a compile-time test cannot know, because how many rows an image spends
// is a property of the image.
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS + IMAGE_GRANT_ROWS <= MIN_GRANT_ROW_BUDGET,
              "a child's mapping quota must fit the smaller profile's free grant rows")

// Every default is under the slab it draws from, which is what makes a child
// meet its OWN budget rather than the machine's. A default at or above its slab
// would make `QuotaExceeded` unreachable for that kind and the row a lie.
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_THREADS < MAX_THREADS, "a thread default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_EVENTS < MAX_EVENTS, "an event default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_WAITERS < MAX_WAITERS, "a waiter default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_INTERRUPTS < MAX_INTERRUPTS, "an interrupt default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_TIMERS < MAX_TIMERS, "a timer default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MEMORIES < MAX_MEMORIES, "a memory default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_IOMEMORIES < MAX_IOMEMORIES, "an iomemory default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_MAPPINGS < MAX_MAPPINGS, "a mapping default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_PIPES < MAX_PIPES, "a pipe default must be under the slab")
static_assert(DEFAULT_QUOTA_HANDLES < MAX_HANDLES, "a handle default must be under the table")
